# Git workflow (single agent, trunk)

Assume **one agent session at a time** on this repo. No parallel agents or
long-lived topic branches unless the user explicitly asks for them.

GitHub Pages deploys from `main`. That is extra reason to keep history linear
and never force-push the default branch unless the user explicitly requests it.

## Trunk and linear history

- Work on the default branch (e.g. `main`). Do not create feature branches for
  routine handoff or page implementation.
- Before continuing after the remote may have moved, use `git pull --rebase`
  (not `git pull` merge) so history stays a straight line.
- Avoid merge commits for integrating your own work.
- Do **not** `git push --force` to the shared default branch unless the user
  explicitly requests it.

## Commit cadence during handoffs

When implementing work described in `docs/handoffs/*.md`, `BLUEPRINT.md`, or
when the user asks you to implement a multi-step change and keep the repo
updated:

1. **Commit often** — after each coherent, working increment
2. **Keep commits small** — one logical concern per commit
3. **Stage narrowly** — explicit paths; do not commit editor junk, `.DS_Store`,
   or unoptimized dumps from `resources/` into `images/` unless asked
4. **Message format** — `.cursor/rules/commit-message.mdc`. Optional handoff
   file name in the body

If the user clearly says **not** to commit, only inspect with `git status` /
`git diff`.

## Session hygiene

End a work block with a **clean** working tree when possible. If you must stop
mid-change, prefer a **WIP commit** with an honest subject (e.g.
`chore(site): wip tokens and eight-circle hero`) unless the user forbids WIP
commits.
